<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- The watch app (PRD-6). Checked in rather than generated because the
	     KVS identifier below is a literal that must NOT be derived from this
	     bundle's own id, and because .gitignore's blanket */*.entitlements
	     rule would otherwise swallow it. -->

	<!-- THE SYNC KEYSTONE. Every other target in this repo writes
	     `$(TeamIdentifierPrefix)$(CFBundleIdentifier)`, which expands to the
	     app's own bundle id and is correct there because all three platforms
	     share `com.couchsuite.nine`. The watch app does not: its bundle id is
	     `com.couchsuite.nine.watchkitapp`, so the same expression would give it
	     a key-value store of its own, and a solve on the wrist would build a
	     streak the phone never sees — silently, forever, with no error
	     anywhere. Pinned to the app's identifier as a literal (PRD-6 §2.5). -->
	<key>com.apple.developer.ubiquity-kvstore-identifier</key>
	<string>$(TeamIdentifierPrefix)com.couchsuite.nine</string>

	<!-- Deliberately absent, each for a reason:

	     • CloudKit. The library does not reach the wrist (PRD-6 §2.5 — boards
	       do not hand off), and declaring `icloud-services` would also oblige
	       `aps-environment` or App Store Connect rejects the upload with error
	       90575. Nothing on the watch would use either.
	     • App groups. The watch's WidgetKit extension is 6b; it is the thing
	       that needs a watch-side group, and it can add one when it exists.
	     • Game Center. The watch reports nothing directly; a solve travels to
	       the phone as a WatchSolveReport and the phone reports it, so the
	       entitlement would buy a capability with no caller. -->
</dict>
</plist>
